Found by the apply worker during k349 (2026-10-31), via bin/ocp --pins-stdin with a wrong PIN1:

    Error: Decryption failed. Wrong password?
    This is an internal error. Run again with --verbose for the full trace.

Cause: OCP::Password (lib/OCP/Password.pm:162) croaks "Decryption failed. Wrong password?". A croak carries " at FILE line N.", which OCP.pm _clean_message classifies as an internal error (lib/OCP.pm:88-90, 253-260). A mistyped PIN is the most common user error; calling it internal sends the user to --verbose for a stack trace instead of retyping.

Done =
- A wrong PIN1 (and PIN2, if it shares the path) dies with a user-facing "message\n": what failed (PIN1 does not open age.key.enc), and the fix (retype; if forgotten, how to recover per docs/security.md). STDERR, exit non-zero, no "internal error" line, no Perl location.
- Same check for the sibling user-facing croaks on the PIN1 path in OCP::Secrets (e.g. _unlock_age_key "No age.key found. Run ocp init first."): list which you converted and which stay croak (programming errors), with why.
- Regression test through bin/ocp --pins-stdin: wrong PIN1 → no "internal error" in STDERR, red first, then a mutation check.

Lane: ocp-secrets-worker (OCP::Password, OCP::Secrets). OCP.pm _clean_message stays as is.
